==English==

===Etymology===
* From {{inh|en|enm|hande mayden}}, {{m|enm|handmaiden}}, {{m|enm|hand mayden}}, {{m|enm|hand-mayden}}, {{m|enm|handmayden}}, {{m|enm|hondemaiden}}, {{m|enm|hond maydyn}}, {{m|enm|hoondmaydyn}}; equivalent to {{compound|en|hand|maiden}}, the first component in the sense of "[[ready]] [[at hand]]".
* {{sense|feminist woman who supports transgender rights}} An allusion to Margaret Atwood's novel ''{{w|The Handmaid's Tale}}'' (1985), in which "handmaids" are women who serve the male commanders in a patriarchal dystopia.

